{"content_hash": "7c517891cf413482", "version_id": 664, "article_id": 67, "level_code": "en_b2", "lang": "en", "level": 4, "level_label": "B2 中高级", "title": "Bank of America fined $1.2bn over money-laundering failures", "title_zh": "美国银行因反洗钱失职被罚12亿美元", "has_translation": true, "topic": "business", "source": "guardian_business", "source_url": "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/14/bank-of-america-fined-money-laundering", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1527874594978-ee4f8a05b4c1?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8MXx8YmFuayUyMGFtZXJpY2ElMjBmaW5lZHxlbnwwfDB8fHwxNzkyMDIyNTY4fDA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Melinda Gimpel on Unsplash", "author": "Melinda Gimpel", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@melindagimpel?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/petition-to-file-for-bankruptcy-9j8k3l9afkc?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(122, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(160, 46%, 48%)", "label": "BUSINESS", "seed": "04b2441ba82d"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-15", "word_count": 244, "reading_minutes": 1.2, "lead": "US regulators have fined Bank of America $1.21bn for failing to detect possible money laundering. The bank says the penalty will reduce its third-quarter earnings.", "lead_zh": "美国监管机构对美国银行开出12.1亿美元罚单，原因是该行未能发现可能的洗钱活动。银行表示，这笔罚款将拉低第三季度利润。", "preview": "The US Treasury Department said the fine is the third-largest in its history. It was imposed because the bank failed to report more than $5b", "preview_zh": "美国财政部表示，这是该部门历史上第三大罚单。处罚原因是该行未能报告超过50亿美元的可疑活动，其中10亿美元涉及国家安全及其他重点非法金融风险。财政部长斯科特·贝森特表示，当如此规模的银行未能履行反洗钱义务时，整个美国金融体系都会面临严重风险。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 649, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/67/en_b2.mp3", "duration": 101.875, "size_bytes": 612144, "engine": "kokoro", "voice": "af_heart", "word_count": 275, "has_timeline": true, "alternates": [{"voice": "af_bella", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/67/en_b2_af_bella.mp3", "duration": 102.975, "size_bytes": 618768, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 275, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/67/en_b2_af_bella.json"}, {"voice": "am_michael", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/67/en_b2_am_michael.mp3", "duration": 108.975, "size_bytes": 654768, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 275, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/67/en_b2_am_michael.json"}, {"voice": "bf_emma", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/67/en_b2_bf_emma.mp3", "duration": 94.65, "size_bytes": 568800, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 275, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/67/en_b2_bf_emma.json"}, {"voice": "bm_george", "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/67/en_b2_bm_george.mp3", "duration": 103.05, "size_bytes": 619200, "engine": "kokoro", "word_count": 275, "has_timeline": true, "timeline_url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/timeline/67/en_b2_bm_george.json"}]}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@2e11152/content/data/versions/664.json", "paragraphs": ["The US Treasury Department said the fine is the third-largest in its history. It was imposed because the bank failed to report more than $5bn in suspicious activity, including $1bn that posed national security and other priority illicit finance risks. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said that when a bank of this size fails to meet its anti-money laundering obligations, the whole US financial system is exposed to serious risks.", "According to the Treasury, the bank prioritised its revenues when building compliance programmes and relied too heavily on outside parties such as law enforcement instead of its own internal controls. Under the consent order, Bank of America must hire an independent third party to review past transactions and report any suspicious activity it failed to detect at the time.", "A Bank of America spokesperson said financial crime compliance is serious and the bank is responsible for getting it right. The bank has invested billions of dollars in technology and thousands of employees to fight financial crime. It added that its third-quarter results will include a $1.2bn charge, reducing net income by about 17 cents a share.", "As part of the penalty, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency levied a $750m civil penalty, its largest ever. The bank also paid an $11.6m fine for over 400 apparent violations of sanctions programmes, including payments related to Russia and Iran. The fine comes days after American Express was hit with a $350m penalty for similar failures."], "paragraphs_zh": ["美国财政部表示，这是该部门历史上第三大罚单。处罚原因是该行未能报告超过50亿美元的可疑活动，其中10亿美元涉及国家安全及其他重点非法金融风险。财政部长斯科特·贝森特表示，当如此规模的银行未能履行反洗钱义务时，整个美国金融体系都会面临严重风险。", "据财政部称，该行在建立合规程序时优先考虑收入，并过度依赖执法部门等外部机构，而不是自身的内部管控。根据同意令，美国银行必须聘请独立第三方审查过往交易，并报告当时未能发现的可疑活动。", "美国银行发言人表示，金融犯罪合规事关重大，银行有责任做好。该行已在技术上投入数十亿美元，并安排数千名员工打击金融犯罪。该行还表示，第三季度业绩将包含12亿美元的费用，使每股净收入减少约17美分。", "作为处罚的一部分，货币监理署对该行处以7.5亿美元民事罚款，这是该监管机构史上最大罚单。该行还因400多起涉嫌违反制裁计划的行为支付了1160万美元罚款，其中包括与俄罗斯和伊朗有关的付款。此次罚款距离美国运通因类似问题被罚3.5亿美元仅数日。"], "body": "The US Treasury Department said the fine is the third-largest in its history. It was imposed because the bank failed to report more than $5bn in suspicious activity, including $1bn that posed national security and other priority illicit finance risks. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said that when a bank of this size fails to meet its anti-money laundering obligations, the whole US financial system is exposed to serious risks.\n\nAccording to the Treasury, the bank prioritised its revenues when building compliance programmes and relied too heavily on outside parties such as law enforcement instead of its own internal controls. Under the consent order, Bank of America must hire an independent third party to review past transactions and report any suspicious activity it failed to detect at the time.\n\nA Bank of America spokesperson said financial crime compliance is serious and the bank is responsible for getting it right. The bank has invested billions of dollars in technology and thousands of employees to fight financial crime. It added that its third-quarter results will include a $1.2bn charge, reducing net income by about 17 cents a share.\n\nAs part of the penalty, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency levied a $750m civil penalty, its largest ever. The bank also paid an $11.6m fine for over 400 apparent violations of sanctions programmes, including payments related to Russia and Iran. The fine comes days after American Express was hit with a $350m penalty for similar failures.", "body_zh": "美国财政部表示，这是该部门历史上第三大罚单。处罚原因是该行未能报告超过50亿美元的可疑活动，其中10亿美元涉及国家安全及其他重点非法金融风险。财政部长斯科特·贝森特表示，当如此规模的银行未能履行反洗钱义务时，整个美国金融体系都会面临严重风险。\n\n据财政部称，该行在建立合规程序时优先考虑收入，并过度依赖执法部门等外部机构，而不是自身的内部管控。根据同意令，美国银行必须聘请独立第三方审查过往交易，并报告当时未能发现的可疑活动。\n\n美国银行发言人表示，金融犯罪合规事关重大，银行有责任做好。该行已在技术上投入数十亿美元，并安排数千名员工打击金融犯罪。该行还表示，第三季度业绩将包含12亿美元的费用，使每股净收入减少约17美分。\n\n作为处罚的一部分，货币监理署对该行处以7.5亿美元民事罚款，这是该监管机构史上最大罚单。该行还因400多起涉嫌违反制裁计划的行为支付了1160万美元罚款，其中包括与俄罗斯和伊朗有关的付款。此次罚款距离美国运通因类似问题被罚3.5亿美元仅数日。", "text": "The US Treasury Department said the fine is the third-largest in its history. It was imposed because the bank failed to report more than $5bn in suspicious activity, including $1bn that posed national security and other priority illicit finance risks. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said that when a bank of this size fails to meet its anti-money laundering obligations, the whole US financial system is exposed to serious risks. According to the Treasury, the bank prioritised its revenues when building compliance programmes and relied too heavily on outside parties such as law enforcement instead of its own internal controls. Under the consent order, Bank of America must hire an independent third party to review past transactions and report any suspicious activity it failed to detect at the time. A Bank of America spokesperson said financial crime compliance is serious and the bank is responsible for getting it right. The bank has invested billions of dollars in technology and thousands of employees to fight financial crime. It added that its third-quarter results will include a $1.2bn charge, reducing net income by about 17 cents a share. As part of the penalty, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency levied a $750m civil penalty, its largest ever. The bank also paid an $11.6m fine for over 400 apparent violations of sanctions programmes, including payments related to Russia and Iran. The fine comes days after American Express was hit with a $350m penalty for similar failures.", "vocab": [{"word": "fine", "pos": "noun", "zh": "罚款", "note": "常与动词 impose/levy 搭配，表示处以罚款", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "money laundering", "pos": "noun", "zh": "洗钱", "note": "指将非法所得合法化的行为", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "suspicious activity", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "可疑活动", "note": "金融监管中常指可能涉及犯罪的交易", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "illicit", "pos": "adj", "zh": "非法的", "note": "比 illegal 更正式，常用于金融和法律语境", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "compliance", "pos": "noun", "zh": "合规", "note": "指遵守法律法规和行业规范", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "consent order", "pos": "noun", "zh": "同意令", "note": "监管机构与机构达成的和解协议", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "penalty", "pos": "noun", "zh": "处罚", "note": "可指罚款、制裁等惩罚措施", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "sanction", "pos": "noun", "zh": "制裁", "note": "常以复数形式出现，指国际制裁措施", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "violation", "pos": "noun", "zh": "违规行为", "note": "指违反法律或规则的行为", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "charge", "pos": "noun", "zh": "费用", "note": "财务语境中指一次性计入的成本", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "net income", "pos": "noun", "zh": "净收入", "note": "扣除所有费用后的利润", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "earnings per share", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "每股收益", "note": "衡量公司盈利能力的重要指标", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Bank of America fined $1.2bn for money-laundering failures", "summary_original": "Third-largest fine in US treasury’s history, due to failure to report suspicious activity, may affect third-quarter earnings\nBank of America\n(BAC.N), has been fined $1.21bn by US regulators for widespread failures to detect ‌potential money laundering and other illicit activity, a penalty the bank said will hurt third-quarter earnings.\nThe US treasury department said the fine, the third-largest in department history, is due to the bank’s failure to report more than $5bn in suspicious activity to the government, including $1bn that posed national security-related and other priority illicit finance risks.\nContinue reading...", "body_original": "Bank of America fined $1.2bn for money-laundering failures\nThird-largest fine in US treasury’s history, due to failure to report suspicious activity, may affect third-quarter earnings\nBank of America\n(BAC.N), has been fined $1.21bn by US regulators for widespread failures to detect ‌potential money laundering and other illicit activity, a penalty the bank said will hurt third-quarter earnings.\nThe US treasury department said the fine, the third-largest in department history, is due to the bank’s failure to report more than $5bn in suspicious activity to the government, including $1bn that posed national security-related and other priority illicit finance risks.\n“Financial institutions are on the ​front lines of protecting our economic and national security,” the treasury secretary, Scott Bessent, said in a statement. “When a bank of this size ​fails to meet its anti-money laundering and sanctions obligations, the consequences extend far beyond its own walls, exposing the ⁠entire US financial system to significant illicit finance risks.”\nThe treasury said the bank “prioritized its revenues” in building compliance programs, and overly relied on inquiries ​from outside parties such as law enforcement and other financial institutions instead of its own internal controls.\nThe consent order requires\nBank of America\nto hire an ​independent third party to review past transactions, identify suspicious activity that the bank failed to detect at the time and report those transactions to the treasury department’s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network.\n“Financial crime compliance is serious, and we are responsible for getting it right,” said a Bank of America spokesperson in a statement. “We have invested billions of dollars in technology and dedicated thousands of ​employees to combat financial crime, and our team is far along with and committed to completing the work required to address the issues raised ​by our regulators.”\nThe spokesperson added that the bank’s third-quarter results due for release on Wednesday would include a non-tax-deductible charge of $1.2bn, which will reduce quarterly net income ‌by approximately ⁠17 cents a share.\nAnalysts have estimated adjusted earnings per share of $1.10, according to mean estimates compiled by LSEG, but that excludes one-offs like this fine.\nShares were down 2.7% in after-hours trading after closing the day down 1.1%.\nThe penalty is likely to add to negative investor sentiment and raise concerns about higher expenses and whether Bank of America can maintain its roughly $6bn quarterly share buyback pace, the UBS analyst Erika Najarian said in a note.\nWith earnings due ​on Wednesday, “we think shares likely underperforming at ​open”, she said.\nShe noted there ⁠was no asset cap, a regulatory penalty that would limit how large the bank could grow.\nAs part of the fine, the treasury department’s office of the comptroller of the currency levied a $750m civil ​penalty against the lender, the largest ever for the regulator, citing failures to address shortcomings in its anti-money ​laundering programs that were ⁠identified as part of a 2024 cease-and-desist order from the watchdog.\nThe regulator said the bank’s compliance program was “deficient and ineffective”, and its transaction monitoring system caused the bank to delay filing suspicious activity reports on billions of dollars of transactions.\nIn addition, the bank paid an $11.6m fine to the treasury department’s office of foreign ⁠assets control ​for more than 400 apparent violations of numerous sanctions programs. The treasury said the bank mistakenly ​processed millions of dollars in payments that violated sanctions programs targeting countries including Russia and Iran.\nThe fine comes days after American Express\nwas also hit with a $350m penalty\nfor similar anti-money ​laundering shortcomings.\nExplore more on these topics\nBank of America\nBanking\nRegulators\nnews\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-15T00:02:46.794735", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.275, "e": 0.388, "sm": 275, "em": 387, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "US", "s": 0.388, "e": 0.8, "sm": 387, "em": 800, "cs": 4, "ce": 6, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "Treasury", "s": 0.8, "e": 1.225, "sm": 800, "em": 1225, "cs": 7, "ce": 15, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "Department", "s": 1.225, "e": 1.937, "sm": 1225, "em": 1937, "cs": 16, "ce": 26, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "said", "s": 1.938, "e": 2.112, "sm": 1937, "em": 2112, "cs": 27, "ce": 31, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "the", "s": 2.112, "e": 2.212, "sm": 2112, "em": 2212, "cs": 32, "ce": 35, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "fine", "s": 2.212, "e": 2.6, "sm": 2212, "em": 2600, "cs": 36, "ce": 40, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "is", "s": 2.6, "e": 2.75, "sm": 2600, "em": 2749, "cs": 41, "ce": 43, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "the", "s": 2.75, "e": 2.9, "sm": 2750, "em": 2899, "cs": 44, "ce": 47, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "third-largest", "s": 2.9, "e": 3.675, "sm": 2900, "em": 3674, "cs": 48, "ce": 61, "si": 0}